    "text": "96\n\n Purchase Agreements: Your (Not-So-Boring) Deal Survival Guide\nHi Sara,\nIf you're buying an accounting firm, you\u2019re going to meet a purchase agreement.\u00a0\nAnd by \u201cmeet,\u201d I mean it\u2019s going to sit on your desk like a 40-page foreign language exam.\nLet\u2019s change that.\nPurchase agreements can look intimidating, but under all that legal lingo, there\u2019s a pattern. A structure. Dare I say... a method to the madness. And understanding it can save you from signing something that quietly sets your hair on fire.\nHere\u2019s the cheat sheet I share with clients:\nArticle 1: What You\u2019re Actually Buying\nAssets, price, terms. It's all in here. Read it like your career depends on it (because it might). Bonus tip: Make sure you understand what's excluded, not just what you're getting. Because if it\u2019s not listed clearly, you\u2019re probably not getting it.\nArticle 3: The Great List of Promises\nThese are the seller\u2019s (and your) reps and warranties. Think of this as the \u201care you telling the truth and nothing but the truth?\u201d section. The hidden magic? These reps force sellers to disclose things they \u201cforgot\u201d to mention\u2014like pending lawsuits or unpaid wage claims. That\u2019s not just legal housekeeping. That\u2019s gold.\nArticle 5: Covenants, aka The Thou Shalt Nots\nThis is where the non-competes, non-solicits, and general behavior clauses live. Are they enforceable? Reasonable? Clear enough that you won\u2019t accidentally trip over them just by breathing in the wrong ZIP code? Let\u2019s hope so.\nArticle 6: Indemnification\nAlso known as \u201cwho\u2019s cleaning up the mess if something goes wrong?\u201d This section matters a lot more than people think, especially when a third party crashes the party with a lawsuit.\nMiscellaneous: The Sneaky Stuff\nBoilerplate doesn\u2019t mean unimportant. I\u2019ve found all kinds of landmines hiding in those standard-seeming sections. Read them. All of them. Yes, even the severability clause.\nSignature Lines: Who\u2019s Actually on the Hook\nMake sure individuals are signing, not just LLCs. You can\u2019t hold \u201cABC Accounting, LLC\u201d accountable when it vanishes six months after closing. You want Jim Anderson\u2019s signature if Jim Anderson is the one making promises.\nStill awake? Then you, my friend, are the exact kind of person I want reading these emails.\nIf this made the fog of legalese lift even a little, and you're thinking \u201cHey, maybe I don\u2019t need to go to law school after all,\u201d you\u2019re welcome.\u00a0\nBut if you want to dig even deeper without the student debt, check out Deal Academy or subscribe to my YouTube channel for more plain-English tips on deal-making done right.\nTo smarter contracts and fewer headaches,\nSara Sharp\nPartner, SK&S Law Group\nFounder, Deal Academy\n\n For more helpful lessons from Deal Academy, check out our Dealmakers Youtube Channel where you'll find Sara's Weekly Wine Down, where she talks about what she's seen in the industry this week and interviews with M&A Experts and Dealmakers.\nYou can check out some recent Dealmakers YouTube videos by clicking the images below.\n\n Copyright \u00a9 2026\u00a0 Someday Consultants, All rights reserved. \n\n Our e-mail address is:\ninfo@dealacademy.org\n\n Want to change how you receive these emails?\n You can unsubscribe from this list.",
